[Scriptoscope](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope) is my project that renders classic Mac OS Kaleidoscope schemes in the browser, from the original 1990s files. This week I wanted to know if it could go the other way: can someone make a *new* Kaleidoscope scheme in 2026, one that loads in the browser and in the real Kaleidoscope on a classic Mac? And where does AI actually help with that?

The answer to the first question is yes. There are now five new schemes, all built in code, and all of them load in the original Kaleidoscope 2.3.1. The work is in [PR #540](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope/pull/540).

The answer to the second question changed twice along the way.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - For the final pixels, ask a model for code or text grids, not pictures. Image generators draw a picture *of* pixel art, on their own grid
> - But image generators were the missing piece for design. My first schemes passed every check and still looked generated. What fixed them was an AI concept image of the whole window, shrunk back to real pixels and cleaned up by hand
> - Booting the original engine found bugs that no check of mine did, including one in Scriptoscope itself

## Why this art is awkward

A Kaleidoscope scheme is a binary resource fork full of tiny colour icons (`cicn` resources) plus recipes that tell the engine how to stretch and tile them into a window frame. Each icon has a 1-bit mask and at most 256 colours, and is drawn at an exact pixel size. A checkbox is 14×14 or 16×16 and nothing else. Some colours, like the title text, are not drawn at all: the engine reads them from hidden pixels at fixed positions.

That is a long way from what image models are built for.

## Round one: asking AI for pixels

I started with the [Google Antigravity CLI](https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/reference) (`agy` 1.4.10), because I already had it and it has an image generator subagent. I asked for a 16×16 PNG checkbox.

<figure class="kh-figure">
  <img class="kh-figure__image" src="/images/blog/pixel-art-agy-raw-checkbox.jpg" width="512" alt="A teal rounded-square checkbox with a white check mark, drawn in a chunky pixel style, sitting on a grey and white checkerboard pattern. Faint smudges of compression noise are visible in the checkerboard near the left and bottom edges.">
  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">What came back, shrunk from 1,024×1,024 for this page. The checkerboard is not transparency; it is painted in.</figcaption>
</figure>

It was a 1,024×1,024 JPEG with about 5,000 colours of compression noise, a checkerboard painted in where the transparency should be, and a box 26 cells wide on a grid of about 36. It also ignored the path I gave it. Two open-source cleanup tools, [unfake](https://github.com/painebenjamin/unfake.py) and [proper-pixel-art](https://github.com/KennethJAllen/proper-pixel-art), could recover *a* grid from it, but never the size I asked for. The model was not misbehaving. Its `generate_image` tool only takes a prompt, a name, an aspect ratio and reference images.

Asking for text worked straight away. With `--json-schema`, `agy` returned a sprite as rows of characters plus a palette, or as drawing code against Scriptoscope's small Raster API, and both came back at the exact size on the first try. Text makes the model commit to every pixel, and what comes back is something I can read, diff and fix. I tried seven of the models `agy` offers on the same checkbox; six passed every automated check, and Claude Opus 5.5 (medium) was the best overall, in about 33 seconds against around 200 for most Gemini runs.

The best route was [claude-pxl](https://github.com/crsolver/claude-pxl), an Agent Skill for "pixel art as code": the model writes a `.px.js` file of palettes and text grids, and the skill builds exact-size PNGs. I gave `agy` its docs as plain context, and Gemini wrote eight control sprites in one go. Every one went through Scriptoscope's encoder and back without a pixel changing.

<figure class="kh-figure">
