</figure>

So my first conclusion was simple: ask for code, not pictures. That still holds for final pixels. It turned out to be only half the story.

## Kingfisher, the first scheme

Alongside the sprite tests, Scriptoscope got an authoring toolkit: encoders for every resource type a scheme needs (each the inverse of a reader Scriptoscope already had), a checker that catches mistakes which load but draw wrong, and a guide. The first scheme made with it began as "Halcyon", a light, frosted look, and is now called Kingfisher. Four AI agents built its control families in parallel: scroll bars; sliders, tabs and popups; progress bars and list headers; and menus, patterns and icons.

To prove the files were real, I copied it into an emulator running System 7.5.5 and loaded it in the original Kaleidoscope 2.3.1. It loaded first time. Once the full scheme was done, the real engine caught a bug my checks had missed: tab labels drawn white on white, because the text colour and the background were read from the same pixel. That is fixed, and there is now a check for it.

