## Letting agents build from the guide

The real test of a guide is whether someone else can follow it. So fresh agents built three more schemes using only the docs, each keeping a log of where it got stuck, and I folded the fixes back into the guide and toolkit between rounds:

- **Castle** came first: merlons along the top, corner towers rising above the title bar, a banner title.
- **Brickworks**, generic toy bricks with a stud row that must never be cut, and **Bonn**, a city skyline, came in the second round.

All three have frames that are not rectangles, which is the hard part of the format. The second-round builders said the first round's fixes worked, and each found new traps, like art that strays one pixel into a tiled cell and then repeats across the whole window.

The most useful finding came from having another agent audit the rounds afterwards. Three fixes had been marked done without taking effect. A lint check promoted from Bonn was never actually called by any scheme. A lesson is not learned until it is applied to the earlier work too.

## They worked, and they looked basic

All four schemes passed every check and loaded in the real Kaleidoscope. Next to the best schemes from the 1990s, like Animals or Evolution, they still looked generated.

A design review pinned it on habits, not on limits of the format. Every surface was outlined and highlighted, so stone read as a grey mesh. Glyphs were drawn with soft strokes thinner than a pixel, which came out as smears. And colours were picked in modern sRGB without checking the 8-bit system palette, so a warm grey turned pink on a real 1990s screen. Underneath all of that, each scheme had been designed one cell at a time, and nothing set the weight of the whole window.

## Concept first, then paint over

What fixed it was designing the whole window as a picture first, which finally gave the image generator a job it was good at.

1. **Write a brief.** An art-director agent wrote one per scheme: the big idea, the one silhouette that carries the theme, the light direction, and a palette on real system-palette colours.
2. **Render a concept.** `agy`'s image generator drew a mockup of the finished window from the brief, with a few 1990s schemes as references for the period look.
3. **Implement it.** Agents rebuilt each scheme against its concept. The first pass copied the motifs and missed the scale: the right towers and birds at sticker size, on the old thin frame. A second pass with numbers measured off the concept (band thicknesses, ornament heights, how far ornaments rise above the bar) fixed the size, but the art still looked redrawn by eye. One pass thickened Castle's walls and shrank its cone roofs into pepper pots, so it read less like a castle than before; a third pass put them back.

