  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">Midnight 2027 (left) and its concept (right), including the concept's invented checkbox labels.</figcaption>
</figure>

Midnight 2027 is the fifth scheme, made for New Year and concept-first from the start. Its concept took five runs; four ended with the subagent cancelled at the timeout and nothing to show for it.

## Back to the real Kaleidoscope

All five repainted schemes loaded in Kaleidoscope 2.3.1 on System 7.5.5 in one boot, switched from the control panel, with no errors. The original engine also behaves like Scriptoscope where it matters. Brickworks tiles a stud row across its title plate, and the seams land at the same offsets from the plate edges in both. Bonn's landmarks sit on the ends of the title in the same places, and both engines drop the title and landmarks together at the same narrow widths.

<figure class="kh-figure">
  <img class="kh-figure__image" src="/images/blog/brickworks-kaleidoscope-wide.png" width="1280" eleventy:formats="png" alt="A classic Mac OS desktop with two windows framed in toy bricks. The front window, titled System Folder, has a red title bar under a row of red studs, walls of stacked red and blue bricks, a green studded baseplate along the bottom and yellow corner bricks, with a yellow crane lifting a red brick above the top right corner. The Finder list view inside shows the Control Panels folder open. A second, inactive window behind it uses paler pink bricks.">
  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">Brickworks in the real Kaleidoscope, with the stud row tiled over the title.</figcaption>
</figure>

There are real differences too. Titles are bitmap Chicago 12. Clicks pass through transparent frame pixels. And art above the title bar goes under the menu bar when a window sits at the top of the screen, which is why the guide now says to keep the essential look in the bar itself.

It found a bug in Scriptoscope as well. Plain dialog and alert bodies were white on the web and grey in the real Kaleidoscope. The grey turned out to come from Kaleidoscope's own 68k system patches, which hand dialogs a colour table from the scheme or, failing that, Kaleidoscope's default. That is fixed on main in [PR #585](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope/pull/585). I would not have found it by reading old schemes; making new ones and comparing them did it.

To check colours before booting, `npm run sim8` simulates an 8-bit classic screen from a browser preview. It picks the same palette entry as the real captures for 92% of frame pixels, and it reproduced every colour problem the first boots found.

## The extras

The Kaleidoscope control panel greys out its "Use This Scheme's" options unless a scheme ships what each needs, and the panel's own help text turned out to be the spec. All five schemes now ship an About This Scheme box, a themed arrow cursor and WindowShade sounds, synthesized in code. With sound off in the emulator I could not hear the sounds, but collapsing a window with them enabled ran without errors.

