## What I am taking from this

Image generators are bad at final pixels and good at concept art. A concept of the whole window gave each scheme the weight and the one memorable silhouette it was missing, and painting over it kept pose and proportion where redrawing by eye lost them. For the exact pixels, code and text grids are still the way: they come out at the right size, and you can read and edit them.

Checks tell you a scheme is valid. They say nothing about whether it is good. Passing every rule I could write did not make the first four look like they belonged next to the 1990s schemes, and the real engine still found bugs those rules missed. Boot the real thing.

Things I have not tried yet: Retro Diffusion and PixelLab, which are the pixel-art models that need paid keys, and feeding the checker's errors straight back to a model in a loop. The authoring guide, the five schemes and the AI notes are in [PR #540](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope/pull/540). If you have a Kaleidoscope scheme you would like to see in a browser, or you made schemes back then and want to try making one now, I would like to hear about it.

